    <div class="calc-notes">
      <h2>How the math works (and where it is rough)</h2>
      <p>The model is deliberately simple so you can interrogate it and adjust. The core relationship: every additional second of mobile load time loses roughly 7&ndash;10% of mobile visitors before they ever read your headline. Google's research, repeatedly confirmed by independent studies, puts the bounce-rate impact in that range.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The PageSpeed delta.</strong> A site at PageSpeed 95+ loads in roughly 1 second on mobile. A site at PageSpeed 50 loads in 4&ndash;5 seconds. The calculator estimates 1.5% additional bounce per PageSpeed point below 95, applied only to the mobile share of visits. That is conservative; the real number can be higher on flaky cellular connections.</li>
        <li><strong>Conversion rate.</strong> Treated as a property of the site, not the platform. The calculator does NOT assume a custom-coded site has a higher conversion rate than your current site &mdash; the only lift is from <em>retaining</em> the visitors who currently bounce off slow load. Real custom sites typically convert higher because of better forms and clearer CTAs, but that lift is not in the calculator. The number you see is the floor.</li>
        <li><strong>Lead-to-close rate.</strong> Held constant. The site does not change the close rate, only the lead volume.</li>
        <li><strong>What is NOT in the model.</strong> Higher local-pack ranking from better schema, organic search visibility from real area pages, retention impact of a maintained site, SEO compounding over time. All of those are real and meaningful; the calculator just does not estimate them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Plain English:</strong> if the number above is bigger than $5K&ndash;$10K, a custom-coded site pays for itself on the leakage line alone. The other factors are upside.</p>
    </div>

